title: 'txpool: hold the transaction pool as a trait object instead of a wrapper'

doc:
  - audience: Node Dev
    description: |-
      `TransactionPoolWrapper` existed only to give the boxed transaction pool a sized type that
      could travel through the generic `Arc<Pool>` parameters used across the service builder. The
      pool is now referenced directly as a trait object and the extra layer of forwarding is gone.

      The trait object and the types it names moved into `sc-transaction-pool-api`

      `FullClientTransactionPool<Block>` and its alias `TransactionPoolHandle<Block>` now live in
      `sc-transaction-pool-api` and carry no `Client` parameter: all of the associated types are
      projections that discard the client, so they are spelled out in terms of `Block` alone. Naming
      them there required moving two types out of `sc-transaction-pool`, which are re-exported from
      their old paths:

      - the pool's error enum, now `sc_transaction_pool_api::error::FullPoolError` (still reachable
        as `sc_transaction_pool::error::Error`),
      - the in-pool transaction, now `sc_transaction_pool_api::Transaction` together with
        `TimedTransactionSource` (still reachable as `sc_transaction_pool::Transaction`).

      Components hold the pool as a trait object rather than a generic

      Everything that merely holds a pool now takes `Arc<TransactionPoolHandle<Block>>` instead of
      being generic over the pool type: the author and `transaction`/`transactionWatch` RPC servers,
      `System` in `substrate-frame-rpc-system`, `ProposerFactory`/`Proposer`, the manual and instant
      seal parameters, `sc-hop`'s `RuntimeApiPromoter`, `sc-mixnet::run`, and `sc-service`'s
      `TransactionPoolAdapter`, `PartialComponents` and metrics. Since these types are no longer
      parameterized by the pool, none of them needs a `?Sized` relaxation, and a component can hold
      a pool with no dependency beyond `sc-transaction-pool-api`.

      Client bounds are only where a pool is built

      `BuildNetworkParams`, `BuildNetworkAdvancedParams`, `SpawnTasksParams` and
      `GenRpcModuleParams` no longer carry a transaction pool type parameter, and none of them
      constrain the client on the pool's behalf any more: the `BlockIdTo` and
      `TaggedTransactionQueue` bounds that only existed to build a pool are gone from
      `build_network`, `build_network_advanced`, `spawn_tasks`, `gen_rpc_module` and from the
      cumulus `build_network`. The capabilities a client needs to actually construct a pool are
      spelled out once as `sc_transaction_pool::ClientForTransactionPool`, used by the transaction
      pool `Builder` and internally by `FullChainApi`, `BasicPool::new_full` and
      `ForkAwareTxPool::new_full`.

      Migration for nodes outside this repository

      - `sc_transaction_pool::Builder::build` hands back an `Arc` already, so drop the surrounding
        `Arc::from(..)` at the construction site.
      - `TransactionPoolHandle<Block, Client>` is `TransactionPoolHandle<Block>` now, and is best
        imported from `sc_transaction_pool_api`.
      - Drop the transaction pool type argument where `BuildNetworkParams`,
        `BuildNetworkAdvancedParams`, `SpawnTasksParams` or `GenRpcModuleParams` are named
        explicitly, since the block type now determines it.
      - In `PartialComponents`, the transaction pool slot is now the block type: pass `Block` where
        `sc_transaction_pool::TransactionPoolHandle<Block>` used to be.
      - A node's own `FullDeps`-style RPC struct no longer needs to be generic over the pool. Hold
        `Arc<TransactionPoolHandle<Block>>` and drop the pool type parameter.

